{
  "thought": "Monetary cost breakdown: compute 52.8%, OS 39.4%, IO 7.9%. The current hardware already has 32 chiplets at max compute_units (16384). The bottleneck is mixed: FFN1 compute-bound (99.9% calc, 2.84s each) and critical-path norm2/res2 memory/communication-bound (50-59% memory, 40-56% communication). The compute-bound FFN1 layers cannot be improved by increasing compute_units (already max). The memory/communication-bound critical-path layers could benefit from DRAM BW or NoP BW increases. IO cost is only 7.9%, so a bandwidth increase would have modest MC impact. However, the historical case (score 0.8895) shows that increasing NoP BW from 128 to 256 on 16 chiplets caused MC to double (237.6 -> 418.3, +76%), worsening objective by 73%. But that was on 16 chiplets with different cost structure. On 32 chiplets, the IO cost share is only 7.9%, so doubling NoP BW would increase IO cost but the total MC impact would be smaller. Let me check the current timeline and critical-path details to understand whether DRAM BW or NoP BW would be more impactful for the critical-path tail layers (norm2, res2, GeLU, res1). I'll build the execution timeline to see the critical path and core utilization.",
  "action": "tool",
  "tool": {
    "name": "build_execution_timeline",
    "arguments": {}
  }
}

